{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVEc3N5UGuvHKZByttHB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`test_large_sequence` fails because the test expects the sequence number to be truncated to 4 digits, and the code never truncates it.\n\n- `invoice.py` formats the number with `f\"{seq:04}\"`. That means \"pad to at least 4 digits.\" So `12345` becomes `\"12345\"`, and the result is `INV-2026-12345`.\n- The test expects `\"INV-2026-2345\"`, which keeps only the last four digits. Nothing in `invoice.py` does that.\n\nThe test's expectation looks wrong, not the code. Truncating `12345` to `2345` would make invoice 12345 get the same number as invoice 2345, so two invoices would share a number. The `:04` in the code is a width, not a cap, and `test_format` (which passes) only checks the padding case.\n\nI haven't changed anything. Two ways to fix it:\n\n1. **Change the test** (my recommendation): expect `\"INV-2026-12345\"`. This matches the code and the padding behavior that `test_format` already covers.\n2. **Change the code**, if sequences really should wrap at 4 digits. Then `invoice_number` needs something like `seq % 10000`, and the wrap causes the collision above. I'd only do this if that is the intended behavior.\n\nWhich one do you want?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":684,"cache_read_input_tokens":41037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":684},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b153a816-8c94-4dbe-830e-4d8a0e70c6b8","uuid":"785b374c-3b68-482f-83e5-fdab835dab1e","timestamp":"2026-10-28T20:04:59.751Z","request_id":"req_011CgVEc35xGvbVWmtwNofZ5"}
